// prism_caps -- capability values: PRISM_SPEC.md §15.2's "Every host power is a value that must
// be threaded from `main`... unforgeable (no constructor exposed), attenuable (`files.read_only()`),
// and revocable (dropping the value revokes it)." Axiom A5. features #112, #124.
//
// WHY THIS IS core/ AND NOT dev/ OR obs/: §15.2's `needs`/`grant` keywords are RESERVED (§3) but
// the compiler-checked "a face that was not granted `camera` cannot open the camera -- the call
// does not type-check" mechanism is UNBUILT (PRISM_STATUS.md: "T11/M0.3, the runtime split" is
// still awaiting owner GO/NO-GO). This module is the VALUE half of §15.2 -- something an
// application can construct, thread through function parameters, attenuate and revoke TODAY -- so
// that application-level capability discipline (explicit `prism_cap_check()` gates before a
// privileged action) is possible before the compiler-enforced half exists. Read the whole of the
// next section before trusting what this module can and cannot promise; the gap between the two
// halves is exactly the gap between "checked by the type system" and "checked at runtime by code
// that chose to call the check."
//
// ── ⛔ WHAT "UNFORGEABLE" CAN ACTUALLY MEAN IN NOVA -- READ THIS BEFORE TRUSTING THIS MODULE ─────
// PRISM_SPEC promises "no constructor exposed." NOVA gives partial, uneven support for that claim,
// and this session measured exactly how far it goes -- both halves are load-bearing for anyone
// building on this module, so both are recorded precisely rather than rounded to "mostly safe."
//
// HALF THAT IS STRUCTURALLY TRUE: a plain struct's POSITIONAL constructor is FILE-LOCAL in NOVA
// (PRISM_STATUS.md MA.3d, independently re-verified with its own 2-file probe: `mod.Struct(...)`
// is a link error E1000, a bare `Struct(...)` in another file is E1002 unknown identifier). So
// `PrismCap(kind, scope, ro, live)` -- the raw constructor -- genuinely CANNOT be called from any
// file but this one. Nobody outside prism_caps.nova can hand-assemble a `PrismCap` from arbitrary
// field values via that route. This is a REAL, compiler-enforced boundary, stronger than anything
// available for a plain function (see prism_event.nova's own honest-limitation note on that).
//
// HALF THAT IS NOT TRUE, AND IS WORSE THAN THE CONVENTIONAL LIMIT PRISM_EVENT DOCUMENTS: NOVA
// auto-generates a `<Type>__from_json(d: dict) -> Type` for EVERY struct (the DB/HTTP serialize
// keystone, zero annotation, `nova_compiler.nova` `_make_from_json_method`) -- and that function is
// an ORDINARY top-level `fn`, not a positional constructor, so it has NORMAL cross-module linkage.
// Measured directly (`_prz_probe6*.nova`, this session, deleted after use): a second file that
// NEVER calls any `prism_cap_grant_*` function can still do
//     let forged = PrismCap__from_json({"cap_kind": "files", "cap_scope": "/", "cap_ro_i": 0, "cap_live": [1]})
// and `prism_cap_is_live(forged)` returns TRUE. The `cap_scope` and `cap_live` fields round-trip
// exactly as the attacker wrote them (plain string/list fields, no validation). The `cap_kind`
// field does NOT round-trip cleanly -- `_make_from_json_method` does not special-case enum-typed
// fields, so the raw JSON string is stuffed into a slot typed `PrismCapKind` without going through
// any of this file's constructors, and reading it back (`prism_cap_kind_name`) produced `""`,
// matching neither arm of an exhaustive match. That is NOT a mitigation -- it means a forged cap's
// *kind* is unpredictable garbage rather than attacker-chosen, which is arguably worse: code that
// pattern-matches `cap_kind` cross-module inherits the OTHER measured NOVA gap (exhaustiveness does
// not check across a module boundary, PRISM_STATUS.md MA.5x) and could silently take a wrong branch
// instead of erroring. Either way, a forged, live, attacker-scoped capability is achievable with NO
// call to any function this file exports, which is a strictly worse hole than "app code can call
// the legitimate grant function without authorization" (prism_event's class of limitation). This is
// a COMPILER gap -- there is no attribute to opt a struct out of `__from_json` generation, and no
// module-private linkage for the function even if there were reason to hide it -- and it is
// out of scope to fix here; `prism/` is the only area this session owns.
//
// WHAT THIS LEAVES THIS MODULE ABLE TO HONESTLY PROMISE: capability values constructed and threaded
// through `prism_cap_grant_*`/`prism_cap_read_only`/`prism_cap_revoke` behave exactly as documented
// below (attenuation cannot widen, revocation propagates to every copy and every attenuated
// derivative). What it CANNOT promise is that every `PrismCap` value a callee receives was
// legitimately granted -- a caller with any code-execution in the process can synthesize one via
// `PrismCap__from_json`. Real unforgeability requires either the compiler-checked `needs`/`grant`
// mechanism §15.2 describes (unbuilt) or a host-boundary check outside NOVA entirely (e.g. the
// actual OS camera permission prompt, which this module does not and cannot replace). Treat
// `prism_cap_check()` below as a DISCIPLINE AID for well-behaved callers, not a security boundary
// against an adversarial one -- exactly PRISM_SPEC's own §15.2 language ("must be threaded from
// main") describes a discipline, and this module makes that discipline possible, not adversarial.
//
// ── ATTENUATION AND REVOCATION -- WHAT ACTUALLY WORKS, AND WHY ──────────────────────────────────
// `cap_live` is a ONE-ELEMENT LIST (`[1]` live / `[0]` revoked), not a plain int field, for a
// specific reason: NOVA has no linear/affine types, so an ordinary struct VALUE can be freely
// copied (assigned, passed, returned) -- "dropping the value revokes it" cannot mean what it says
// literally, because there is no way to stop a copy from continuing to exist. A LIST, however, is a
// reference type: copying the STRUCT copies the list HANDLE, not the list's contents, so every copy
// of a `PrismCap` -- and critically, every value `prism_cap_read_only()` DERIVES from it, which
// deliberately shares the SAME `cap_live` list rather than allocating a fresh one -- observes the
// SAME liveness cell. `prism_cap_revoke(cap)` mutates that cell in place (`cap.cap_live[0] = 0`),
// which is therefore visible through every copy and every attenuation, without needing module-level
// mutable state (itself a fragile pattern in this codebase -- see prism_node.nova, prism_theme.nova
// and others' own notes on module-level `let` collections). This is the standard mechanism real
// capability-security systems use for revocation without linear types: a capability is a HANDLE
// checked against shared live state at USE time, not a value whose mere non-existence enforces
// anything (seL4's revocation via capability-derivation trees and the object-capability
// literature's "Revoker" idiom both work this way). Attenuation sharing the parent's cell rather
// than minting a fresh one is deliberate: it means revoking a root grant also kills everything
// derived from it, and there is no "re-widen" function that could hand attenuation back its
// original power -- the only way to get a full-power cap is `prism_cap_grant_*` itself, which is
// exactly as available (or as forgeable) as it always was.
//
// PUBLIC API
//   prism_cap_kind_all()                          -> list<PrismCapKind>   the closed vocabulary
//   prism_cap_kind_name(k)                        -> string
//   prism_cap_grant(kind, scope)                  -> Result<PrismCap>     generic grant
//   prism_cap_grant_camera/_embed/_clipboard/_geo/_mic/_notify()  -> Result<PrismCap>  (unscoped)
//   prism_cap_grant_files/_network/_storage(scope) -> Result<PrismCap>    (scope required)
//   prism_cap_read_only(cap)                      -> Result<PrismCap>     attenuate; err if not live
//   prism_cap_revoke(cap)                         -> PrismCap             mutates the shared cell
//   prism_cap_is_live(cap) / prism_cap_is_read_only(cap)  -> bool
//   prism_cap_kind(cap) / prism_cap_scope(cap)
//   prism_cap_check(cap)                          -> Result<PrismCap>     the §15.2 gate
//   prism_cap_describe(cap)                       -> string               hand-written, safe

// The starter vocabulary: `camera`, `files` and `embed` are the three PRISM_SPEC.md names this
// vocabulary is anchored to (§15.2's example, §17's escape hatch). The remaining six are the
// obviously-necessary host powers any real UI framework needs and are NOT yet spec-ratified --
// PRISM_SPEC.md line 922 tracks "minimum viable capability set for v1" as an OPEN QUESTION for
// Phase 3. Extending this enum later is additive and, because the exhaustive `prism_cap_kind_name`
// match below lives in THIS file beside the enum, a missed arm is a same-file compile error
// (E1009) -- the one place in this module exhaustiveness checking is real (PRISM_STATUS.md MA.5x:
// it does NOT hold across a module boundary, so no caller elsewhere should assume the same).
enum PrismCapKind
    CapCamera()
    CapFiles()
    CapEmbed()
    CapNetwork()
    CapStorage()
    CapClipboard()
    CapGeo()
    CapMic()
    CapNotify()

// `cap_` prefix on every field -- NOVA's struct field->slot map is GLOBAL (see prism_secret.nova
// and prism_event.nova for the same note); an unprefixed `kind`/`scope`/`live` would collide.
type PrismCap
    cap_kind: PrismCapKind
    cap_scope: string
    cap_ro_i: int
    cap_live: list

fn prism_cap_kind_camera() -> PrismCapKind
    CapCamera()

fn prism_cap_kind_files() -> PrismCapKind
    CapFiles()

fn prism_cap_kind_embed() -> PrismCapKind
    CapEmbed()

fn prism_cap_kind_network() -> PrismCapKind
    CapNetwork()

fn prism_cap_kind_storage() -> PrismCapKind
    CapStorage()

fn prism_cap_kind_clipboard() -> PrismCapKind
    CapClipboard()

fn prism_cap_kind_geo() -> PrismCapKind
    CapGeo()

fn prism_cap_kind_mic() -> PrismCapKind
    CapMic()

fn prism_cap_kind_notify() -> PrismCapKind
    CapNotify()

fn prism_cap_kind_all() -> list
    [CapCamera(), CapFiles(), CapEmbed(), CapNetwork(), CapStorage(), CapClipboard(), CapGeo(), CapMic(), CapNotify()]

fn prism_cap_kind_name(k: PrismCapKind) -> string
    match k
        CapCamera() => "camera"
        CapFiles() => "files"
        CapEmbed() => "embed"
        CapNetwork() => "network"
        CapStorage() => "storage"
        CapClipboard() => "clipboard"
        CapGeo() => "geo"
        CapMic() => "mic"
        CapNotify() => "notify"

// Which kinds carry a meaningful scope (a path/host/key prefix that NARROWS the grant) vs which
// are all-or-nothing host powers with no sub-scoping concept.
fn _prism_cap_kind_scoped(k: PrismCapKind) -> bool
    match k
        CapFiles() => true
        CapNetwork() => true
        CapStorage() => true
        CapCamera() => false
        CapEmbed() => false
        CapClipboard() => false
        CapGeo() => false
        CapMic() => false
        CapNotify() => false

// A scoped kind REQUIRES a non-empty scope (an unscoped "files" grant is unbounded filesystem
// access by omission, not by a deliberate empty-string choice -- reject rather than guess). An
// unscoped kind REJECTS a non-empty scope rather than silently discarding it, so a caller who
// passes a path to `camera` gets told, not ignored.
fn _prism_cap_check_scope(k: PrismCapKind, scope) -> Result<string>
    if type_of(scope) != "string"
        return err("prism_caps: scope must be a string, got {type_of(scope)}")
    let scoped = _prism_cap_kind_scoped(k)
    if scoped and len(scope) == 0
        return err("prism_caps: {prism_cap_kind_name(k)} requires a non-empty scope (a path/host/key prefix) -- an unscoped grant of this kind is unbounded access by omission")
    if scoped == false and len(scope) > 0
        return err("prism_caps: {prism_cap_kind_name(k)} does not take a scope, got '{scope}'")
    ok(scope)

// prism_cap_grant: the one place a fresh, full-power, LIVE capability comes from. See the file
// header for exactly what "the one place" can and cannot mean in NOVA.
fn prism_cap_grant(k: PrismCapKind, scope: string) -> Result<PrismCap>
    let s = _prism_cap_check_scope(k, scope)?
    ok(PrismCap(k, s, 0, [1]))

fn prism_cap_grant_camera() -> Result<PrismCap>
    prism_cap_grant(CapCamera(), "")

fn prism_cap_grant_files(path_prefix: string) -> Result<PrismCap>
    prism_cap_grant(CapFiles(), path_prefix)

fn prism_cap_grant_embed() -> Result<PrismCap>
    prism_cap_grant(CapEmbed(), "")

fn prism_cap_grant_network(host_prefix: string) -> Result<PrismCap>
    prism_cap_grant(CapNetwork(), host_prefix)

fn prism_cap_grant_storage(key_prefix: string) -> Result<PrismCap>
    prism_cap_grant(CapStorage(), key_prefix)

fn prism_cap_grant_clipboard() -> Result<PrismCap>
    prism_cap_grant(CapClipboard(), "")

fn prism_cap_grant_geo() -> Result<PrismCap>
    prism_cap_grant(CapGeo(), "")

fn prism_cap_grant_mic() -> Result<PrismCap>
    prism_cap_grant(CapMic(), "")

fn prism_cap_grant_notify() -> Result<PrismCap>
    prism_cap_grant(CapNotify(), "")

// prism_cap_read_only: attenuate. Cannot be undone -- there is no "widen" function, and the
// returned value shares the PARENT's cap_live cell (see file header), so revoking the parent also
// revokes every attenuation derived from it. Attenuating an already-revoked cap is rejected: a
// revoked capability has no power left to narrow.
fn prism_cap_read_only(cap: PrismCap) -> Result<PrismCap>
    if prism_cap_is_live(cap) == false
        return err("prism_caps: cannot attenuate a revoked {prism_cap_kind_name(cap.cap_kind)} capability")
    ok(PrismCap(cap.cap_kind, cap.cap_scope, 1, cap.cap_live))

// prism_cap_revoke: mutate the shared liveness cell. Visible through every copy of `cap` and every
// value `prism_cap_read_only` derived from it, per the file header. Returns `cap` back (now dead)
// for chaining convenience; it is the SAME cell, not a new one, so there is nothing further to do.
fn prism_cap_revoke(cap: PrismCap) -> PrismCap
    cap.cap_live[0] = 0
    cap

fn prism_cap_is_live(cap: PrismCap) -> bool
    cap.cap_live[0] == 1

fn prism_cap_is_read_only(cap: PrismCap) -> bool
    cap.cap_ro_i == 1

fn prism_cap_kind(cap: PrismCap) -> PrismCapKind
    cap.cap_kind

fn prism_cap_scope(cap: PrismCap) -> string
    cap.cap_scope

// prism_cap_check: the §15.2 gate a privileged action calls before proceeding. A Result forces the
// caller to handle a dead capability, where a bool getter invites being ignored -- same reasoning
// as prism_event's `prism_ev_require_user`.
fn prism_cap_check(cap: PrismCap) -> Result<PrismCap>
    if prism_cap_is_live(cap)
        ok(cap)
    else
        err("prism_caps: {prism_cap_kind_name(cap.cap_kind)} capability (scope='{cap.cap_scope}') is revoked")

// prism_cap_describe: hand-written, deliberately not relying on the generic str()/to_json()
// builtins -- prism_secret.nova's header documents a measured NOVA defect where those mis-dispatch
// for an imported struct once the caller's file declares any struct of its own. Nothing here is
// secret-valued, so there is no redaction concern, only the same dispatch-safety reason to hand-roll it.
fn prism_cap_describe(cap: PrismCap) -> string
    let live_s = if prism_cap_is_live(cap)
        "live"
    else
        "revoked"
    let ro_s = if prism_cap_is_read_only(cap)
        " read_only"
    else
        ""
    "{prism_cap_kind_name(cap.cap_kind)} scope='{cap.cap_scope}' {live_s}{ro_s}"
